WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
we can speak now to the deputy secretary  general of the national education union  neve sweeney thank you so much for being  on the programme  do you feel like a teacher strike is  inevitable  i think it's more likely than it's been  in my 20 years of working in the  profession uh if you know even before  the pandemic teachers were working more  unpaid over time than any other  profession and when early career  teachers calculate their hourly pay  they're often being paid under the  living wage as a graduate profession the  pay simply hasn't kept up with with  other uh graduate professions and  teachers are saying to us  that they are finding it difficult to  get to the end of the month their  heating bills and their fuel bills mean  that they are struggling to survive  uh yeah i mean look look i think don't  think anyone would say that times are  easy particularly of course for people  who've had you know years of  pay restraint  i'm just interested to know exactly what  you're calling for so you say you want  an inflation plus pay rise inflation of  course is currently at 9.1 percent  what is the pay rise that you want to  see  well at the moment we think that the  strv is likely to recommend to the  secretary of state about a three three  percent pay increase now  with cpi or rpi that means an actual pay  cut for our members of between six and  eight percent and  of austerity and pay restraint and last  year's pay freeze so the average teacher  will have lost between 70 and 17 and 20  in real terms of their wages so if they  don't offer something that is more than  at least cpi inflation the majority of  teachers will be facing another year of  a pay cut  so let's just be really really clear  then on what you're calling for uh  inflation is currently at 9.1 percent so  so what it just spell out for us and  what is it that you want to see  we've said to nadeem sahabi today that  he needs to  very seriously consider an above  inflation pay rise for teachers across  the board and it needs to be fully  funded so we need to make sure that  schools are able to pay this because  what we're seeing is a teacher  recruitment and retention crisis one in  four teachers leaves the profession  within two years and one in three  teachers leaves within five and that  means that children and young people are  less likely  you want to see pay rise with over 9.1  that's correct is it we have to make  sure that teachers aren't having another  year of a pay cut and that's what  they've been suffering for the last 10  years plus the pay freeze that was  issued last year to all of you what  exactly are you calling for  so we we we have said that we're calling  for an above  above inflation pay pay fully funded pay  rise for all teachers and all education  workers we mustn't forget that support  staff uh you know support staff wages  have not kept up with those of retail or  hospitality and support staff members in  our schools work with some of our most  vulnerable children and those with  special educational need and disability  if the government doesn't address  teacher pay then it is going to face an  even bigger workforce crisis than it is  in at the moment and what we are seeing  is children in secondary school having  three or four maths teachers within a  year i've got a friend who's a physics  teacher who started after easter and  every time he comes to the end of a  lesson the children say to him sir are  you coming back next week children need  a qualified experienced teacher who is  committed to the profession that isn't  worried about whether they can pay their  mortgage that doesn't you know isn't  worried about whether they can uh fill  their car with petrol at the end of the  month and isn't facing two thousand  pound hike in their own child care fees  you know we're a profession of 76 women  who have suffered through austerity and  have suffered through the through the  pandemic there's a pay gap in a pension  gap and children and families and  schools deserve better okay um so pretty  clear then that you're going to be  balancing a fraction you want to see an  above inflation payroll say reading  between the lines of what you said  that's effectively double figures above  9.1 percent  so my other question is you know  children have lost so much education  time already because of the pandemic and  we know that it's the most deprived  children who've suffered because of that  you know according to the southern trust  social mobility fell by 12  thanks to the lost learning uh during  doing the pandemic  how comfortable are you that if teachers  do go on strike that's going to be more  lost hours for children in the classroom  and more disruption particularly you  know for those underprivileged children  teachers are acutely aware of the impact  that them taking strike action would  have on children that's why they would  never take it lightly and that's why you  know it's taken this crisis for for them  to be considering it but i've spoken to  more teachers this week who have said  that they would for the first time in  their career consider taking strike  action because they recognize that it's  not just about the pay in their pockets  it's about recruiting graduate teachers  into the profession and keeping teachers  in the profession so that children have  a well-qualified experienced teacher  teaching teaching them  we've seen you're right the children who  are most deprived you know it's often  the fact that they are coming from uh  families that the educational  disadvantage is already in place before  they reach school so if the government  is really  really you know true to its word about  leveling up it will invest in early  years education and invest in fully  funded pay increase that keeps teachers  in the classroom  okay um i've spoken to mitt lynch of the  rmt at the weekend who said that they're  effectively prepared to keep striking  until  they get what they are asking for when  it comes to pay  i just wonder if that's the same for  teachers how long could the strikes last  for  well we haven't had a pay offer from the  employer yet so we are hoping that the  department of education that is in tsar  we would read our letter today we'll  hear the news stories and all the  interviews of teachers that i've heard  on the news and regulations how long the  question is sorry to just to try and you  know  re get back to the question but the  question is how long could the strikes  last for  well we haven't even balloted yet sophie  so that's why we're putting this warning  out this is the strength of the feeling  and the crisis of the cost of living  that we are in right now we have said  that we we are committed from our  conference in uh easter to run an  indicative ballot in the autumn term and  we will be recommending to our members  that they vote yes for strike action  that's a warning stand i understand that  we're early in the process but i know  that  i know that you know parents will be  i know that parents will be like really  concerned about the idea of uh staff  going on strike even though of course  many i guess would understand the desire  for better pay you know were we talking  days are we talking weeks are we talking  months or do we just not know  that will be down to our members and  what they decide they are willing and  prepared to do  but i tell you what parents are also  concerned about parents are concerned  that teacher vacancies are up by 14  teachers are parents are concerned that  their children are their class sizes are  rising because classes are having to be  merged because teacher vacancies aren't  being filled and parents are concerned  that their children are being taught by  non-subject specialists we're finding it  more difficult to recruit i.t teachers  teachers that need to develop curriculum  that's vital for our economy and vital  for young people entering into the  workforce and the next phases phases of  their educational career that's what  teachers our parents are are extremely  worried about and nadim zahawi needs to  hear that call and needs to ensure that  teacher pay maintains its its balance  with other graduate professions and that  we are getting the right number of  teachers to come into the profession and  keeping teachers once they get there

